Edge-based distributed deep learning (DDL) is increasingly important for embedded AI scenarios where data resides within a trusted domain (e.g., a hospital or campus network) and compute is pushed to edge nodes for cost or performance reasons. Deploying efficient DDL systems in such settings requires evaluating a large space of training configurations, each defined by a combination of cluster topology and synchronization strategy. Exploring this space through real training is prohibitively slow, and existing single-machine simulators are inaccurate and inefficient, as they require full training runs to estimate timing, overlook communication effects, and suffer timing distortions due to host-level scheduling. We present ETSim, a system that enables efficient and accurate design-time exploration of edge DDL training configurations on a single machine. ETSim uses a two-phase design that decouples timing simulation from convergence modeling. In Phase 1, it performs lightweight discrete-event simulation to generate a schedule of computation and communication events based on device and network capabilities. In Phase 2, it replays the computation events using actual deep learning operations to simulate convergence behavior under the system dynamics captured in Phase 1. This design allows rapid pruning of suboptimal configurations while preserving fidelity in the evaluation of promising ones. Our evaluation shows that ETSim achieves over 92% accuracy in training time estimation compared to a real 16-node edge testbed, while running up to 10,000 × faster. We demonstrate its utility through case studies that show how ETSim guides topology selection, synchronization choices, and scaling decisions for edge-deployed DDL training.